---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CadViewer from '../components/CadViewer.tsx';
---
<Base
  title="CAD viewer — Sharp AI"
  description="View the team's exported robot CAD in the browser. Drag in a glTF or STL export from Onshape; nothing is uploaded."
>
  <section class="wrap prose-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">CAD</p>
    <h1>Look at the robot.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Export a part, an assembly or the whole robot from Onshape and look at it here,
      on our own site, without an Onshape account or a CAD install. Useful for design
      review, for a new member who has not been given access yet, and for arguing about
      a mechanism from a phone.
    </p>

    <CadViewer client:only="react" />

    <h2>Why this rather than an embedded Onshape</h2>
    <p>
      Onshape is proprietary, and its integration story runs the other way: it supports
      putting an app <em>inside</em> Onshape, not putting Onshape inside another site.
      Nothing here is a copy of it. The geometry is ours, so exporting it and showing it
      under our own branding is straightforwardly allowed — and it works for anyone with
      a browser, which an embed would not.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting a model out of Onshape</h2>
    <p>
      Right-click a Part Studio or Assembly tab and choose <strong>Export</strong>, with
      the format set to <strong>glTF</strong> or <strong>STL</strong>. Drop the file on the
      view above and it appears immediately. Nothing is uploaded — the file is read in your
      browser and never leaves the machine, so an unreleased design stays private.
    </p>
    <p>
      To put a model on this page for the whole team, commit it to
      <code>web/public/cad/</code> and add an entry to <code>manifest.json</code>. There is
      also <code>scripts/onshape-export.mjs</code>, which pulls an export straight from the
      Onshape API if you have developer keys.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
